course: everyday
lesson: 334
title: Harmful pranks, April Fools' tricks, hidden adverts and too-good-to-be-true offers
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A good prank leaves everyone laughing, including the one it was played on. A prank that damages things, like egging a house or stuffing a toilet, means someone has to clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime. Animals never understand pranks: they just get frightened, stressed or hurt.
explain: On April 1st news websites and companies often publish joke stories; in 1957 the BBC famously showed spaghetti «growing on trees». So on April Fools' Day you check the date and think. But nobody should ever pretend to be hurt, missing or in danger as a joke.
explain: Adverts show a product at its very best, sometimes with special effects, which is why they say «pictures are for illustration». Influencers are often paid to praise products, so look for #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership». A brand of soda in a film is often product placement: the company paid for it to be there. A loot box, where you pay without knowing what you get, works like gambling.
explain: An offer that sounds too good to be true usually is: quick money for no work, famous trainers for a tiny price on a strange website, or a stranger offering sweets or a puppy if you come with them. Never go anywhere with someone you do not know; run to a safe adult and tell.
fact: Never damage property in a prank; someone has to clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime.
fact: Never prank animals; they do not understand jokes and get frightened, stressed or hurt.
fact: On April Fools' Day, news and companies often publish joke stories, so check the date and think.
fact: Never pretend to be hurt, missing or in danger as a joke; it causes real fear and can waste emergency services' time.
fact: Adverts show toys at their best, sometimes with effects, and the real toy may be smaller or do less.
fact: «Pictures are for illustration» means the picture may not show exactly what you get.
fact: Influencers are often paid to praise products; #ad, «sponsored» or «paid partnership» shows it.
fact: A loot box is like gambling: you pay without knowing what you get, and games make it exciting so you spend more.
fact: Product placement is when a company pays for its product to appear in a film or show; it is advertising hidden inside entertainment.
fact: Real money takes real work, and «get rich quick» offers are almost always tricks.
fact: Fake shops copy brand names, sell things that never arrive and steal card details, so a parent should check a strange website.
fact: Offers of sweets or a puppy are a known trick to lure children; never go anywhere with someone you do not know.
exercise: Some older kids plan to throw eggs at a neighbour's house as a prank. Why is this wrong? | Someone has to clean or pay for the damage, and it can be a crime | It is only wrong if the neighbour sees them and calls their parents | Eggs are too expensive to waste | It is fine if it is Halloween
exercise: Which prank is never acceptable? | Stuffing a school toilet so it floods | Putting a plastic spider in a friend's lunchbox | Telling a silly made-up fact and then saying «Joke!» | Saying «Look, a pink elephant!» and laughing together
exercise: A boy wants to film himself scaring the family cat with a loud noise. Why should he not? | Animals do not understand jokes; they get frightened, stressed or hurt | Cats always enjoy surprises and like to play chasing games | It is fine if the video is funny | Cats forget scares straight away
exercise: On April 1st a news website says the school holidays will be cancelled forever. What should you do first? | Check the date and think whether it is a joke | Believe it because it is on a real news website | Panic and tell everyone it is true | Share it at once with the whole class
exercise: What was the famous BBC April Fools' report of 1957 about? | Spaghetti growing on trees | Cows that could fly over farms | A talking dog reading the news | Snow falling in the middle of summer
exercise: A girl wants to text her mum on April Fools' Day that she has broken her leg. Why is this a bad joke? | It causes real fear and could even bring out help that is not needed | It is only a bad joke if she sends it twice or forgets to say «April Fool» | Mums always know it is April 1st | Broken legs are too common to be funny
exercise: An advert shows a toy car flying around a room. The box says «pictures are for illustration». What does this tell you? | The real toy may be smaller or do less than in the advert | The toy will fly exactly like that once you take it home and charge it | The picture was drawn by a child | Every toy in the shop can fly
exercise: Why can a toy look better in an advert than in real life? | Adverts show it at its best, sometimes with special effects | The advert shows a different, older toy from many years ago | Toys get smaller in the shop | Toys only work on television
exercise: A YouTuber says she loves a new snack, and her video says «paid partnership». What does that mean? | She was paid by the company to talk about it | She bought it with her own money and loved it | It is the healthiest snack there is | Her fans paid for the video
exercise: In a game, you can pay real money for a loot box that might hold a rare prize. Which is TRUE? | It is like gambling: you pay without knowing what you get | You always get the rare prize in the end if you keep paying | It is the same as buying a toy in a shop | Loot boxes are free gifts from the game
exercise: The hero of a film drinks a can of a famous soda with the label turned to the camera. What is this most likely? | Product placement: the company paid to show it | A lucky accident during filming that nobody noticed | The actor's own favourite drink | A warning not to drink soda
exercise: A stranger online says you can earn 500 dollars a day just by clicking. What should you do? | Ignore it and tell an adult, because it is almost surely a trick | Click quickly before the offer ends, because good offers never come back | Send your name and address to find out more | Ask a friend to try it first
exercise: Famous trainers are on sale for a tiny price on a website you have never heard of. What is sensible? | Ask a parent to check it, because it may be a fake shop | Buy them at once with a parent's card before they sell out | Give the website your address to reserve a pair | Tell everyone at school about the bargain
exercise: A friendly-looking stranger in a park says, «Come to my car and see my puppies». What should a child do? | Never go, run to a safe adult and tell | Go quickly and come straight back | Go if the stranger seems kind | Ask the stranger to bring a puppy closer
test: Which prank is fine to play? | A silly joke the friend laughs at too, with nothing broken | Hiding a dog's food bowl to watch it panic and run about | Letting down a neighbour's car tyres | Writing on a classroom wall
test: A boy says, «It's just a prank» after kicking over the bins in his street. Which is TRUE? | Damaging or making a mess for others is not a harmless prank | It is fine if nobody saw him, because then nobody can be upset | Pranks never count as doing wrong | It only matters if the bins break
test: A story says a famous zoo will let penguins fly around the city from tomorrow. It is April 1st. What is the most likely explanation? | It is an April Fools' joke story | The zoo has trained flying penguins | The news has made an honest mistake | Penguins learn to fly in the spring
test: Why would a company pay to put its chocolate bar in a cartoon film? | Children see it and want to buy it | The film needs snacks for the actors | Cartoons are not allowed to show other food | The company wants to keep it a secret
test: An influencer in Australia posts «Best game EVER! #ad». What should you think? | She may have been paid, so her praise is not a neutral opinion | It must be the best game ever, since she plays games all day long and knows | #ad means the game is free | #ad means she tested it for a whole year
test: A boy in Texas spends all his birthday money on loot boxes hoping for a rare skin and gets none. Why does this happen to many players? | Loot boxes work like gambling, and the game makes them exciting to spend more | He was very unlucky; loot boxes always pay off eventually | The game made a mistake | He should simply have bought more boxes
test: A message says, «You've won a phone! Pay 5 pounds for delivery». Which is TRUE? | It is almost certainly a trick to take money or card details | It is safe because 5 pounds is a small amount and the phone is worth a lot | Small delivery fees prove it is real | Prizes always come by message
test: A stranger at the playground offers a girl sweets if she helps him look for his lost puppy. What should she do? | Say no, go straight to a safe adult and tell | Help him look for a few minutes, then go back | Take the sweets and stay where she is | Ask him to describe the puppy first
test: A toy robot on TV dances and talks. At home, it only rolls forward. Why did this happen? | The advert showed it at its best, sometimes with effects | The TV made the robot better while it was being filmed in the studio | The toy was broken in the shop | Robots forget their dances at home
explain: A teenager in Canada filmed himself hiding a fake spider in a friend's car; the friend panicked while driving and crashed into a fence.
explain: A boy in England paid for an «influencer's» favourite gadget after a video; it was a paid advert, and the gadget broke in a week.
fact: Pranks that startle someone who is driving, cooking or on stairs can cause real accidents.
fact: Influencers are often paid to praise things, so their praise is an advert, not a test; check for #ad and read reviews from ordinary buyers.
test: lets scare my brother while he's on the stairs | Never; he could fall and get hurt | Do it, it's funny, he'll laugh after | Do it at the bottom step | Do it if he's holding the rail
test: youtuber says best headphones ever, buy them now? | Check if it's #ad and read real reviews | Buy now, he knows best | Buy two | Buy if he has many fans
test: april fool, tell my teacher my mum's in hospital | Never pretend someone's hurt; it scares people | It's fine, it's April Fools, everyone knows it's a joke | It's fine if you say it fast | It's fine if mum agrees
test: did u hear some kid jumped out and scared his dad while he was carrying a hot pan and "it went everywhere, burns all up his arm". lesson? | Never startle someone who is cooking, driving or on stairs | Scare pranks are fine if they laugh after | Only scare people when they're sitting | Dads never drop things
test: my cousin says his mate's influencer "really uses that cream every day, it's not an ad". what's likely | Influencers are often paid; look for #ad or «sponsored» | If they smile and use it on camera, it's not an ad | Influencers never take money | Everything influencers say is tested
test: kids say the boy who egged a car on April Fools "is a criminal now, the police took him". pass it on? | No, it's a rumour; just never damage property in a prank | Yes, he's a criminal | Yes, and add what he did next | Yes, everyone should know
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises pranks that damage property? | Someone must clean or pay for the damage, and they may be a crime | They are harmless if done on April Fools' Day, when such jokes are customary | They are permitted if nobody is hurt | They are the responsibility of the owner
exam: Identify the principal danger of startling a person who is driving, cooking or on the stairs. | It can cause real accidents | It merely annoys them a little | It improves their reactions to danger | It has no effect at all on adults
exam: It is given that a stranger offers a child sweets or a puppy to go with them. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | The child shall not go and shall run to a safe adult | The child may go if the puppy is visible | The child may go if a friend comes too | The child shall go if the stranger is polite
exam: State the meaning of praise by an influencer who is paid by a company. | It is an advert, not a test of the product | It is an independent scientific review | It is a guarantee of quality | It is an official government approval
